Subject: [PATCH] [mlir][dataflow] Initialize the sparse and dense analyses from a worklist

The sparse forward and backward analyses initialize through
`initializeRecursively`, and the dense forward and backward analyses
through `initialize` itself, each calling itself once for every
operation of every block, so the native stack an analysis needs grows
with the nesting depth of the IR: SCCP, the integer range analysis,
liveness and every dense analysis overflow the stack of a thread with a
small one on a deeply nested function.

`DataFlowAnalysis::initializeNested` visits an operation and every
operation and block nested in it in the order those recursions took (an
operation before its regions, each block before its operations, and the
operations of a block forward or, for a backward analysis, last first,
each with everything nested in it before the next), keeping its position
in an explicit worklist. The four initializations call it with what they
did per operation and per block, so every state is created, subscribed
and visited in the same order as before. A dense analysis's nested
operations were initialized through the virtual `initialize`, so an
override of it in a dense analysis was called on each of them; it is now
called on the top operation alone, as it is for the sparse analyses. No
analysis in the tree overrides it.

A unit test runs dead-code analysis, constant propagation, liveness and
a dense forward and a dense backward analysis over a chain of operations
nested 10,000 deep on a thread whose stack a frame per level would
overflow, and each of them reaches the innermost operation.
---
